<refentry id="sql-refreshmaterializedview">
 <indexterm zone="sql-refreshmaterializedview">
  <primary>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>SQL - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW</refname>
  <refpurpose>replace the contents of a materialized view</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW [ CONCURRENTLY ] <replaceable class="parameter">name</replaceable>
    [ WITH [ NO ] DATA ] [ WHERE <replaceable class="parameter">condition</replaceable> ]
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW</command> completely replaces the
   contents of a materialized view.  To execute this command you must have the
   <literal>MAINTAIN</literal>
   privilege on the materialized view.  The old contents are discarded.  If
   <literal>WITH DATA</literal> is specified (or defaults) the backing query
   is executed to provide the new data, and the materialized view is left in a
   scannable state.  If <literal>WITH NO DATA</literal> is specified no new
   data is generated and the materialized view is left in an unscannable
   state.
  </para>
  <para>
   <literal>CONCURRENTLY</literal> and <literal>WITH NO DATA</literal> may not
   be specified together.
  </para>
  <para>
   If a <literal>WHERE</literal> clause is specified, only the rows matching
   the <replaceable class="parameter">condition</replaceable> are updated.
   Rows in the materialized view that match the condition but are no longer
   present in the underlying base tables (or no longer match the query definition)
   are deleted. New rows from the base tables that match the condition are inserted.
   Rows in the materialized view that do not match the condition are left unchanged.
   The <literal>WHERE</literal> clause cannot be used with <literal>WITH NO DATA</literal>.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>CONCURRENTLY</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Refresh the materialized view without locking out concurrent selects on
      the materialized view.  Without this option a refresh which affects a
      lot of rows will tend to use fewer resources and complete more quickly,
      but could block other connections which are trying to read from the
      materialized view.  This option may be faster in cases where a small
      number of rows are affected.
     </para>
     <para>
      This option is only allowed if there is at least one
      <literal>UNIQUE</literal> index on the materialized view which uses only
      column names and includes all rows;  that is, it must not be an
      expression index or include a <literal>WHERE</literal> clause.
     </para>
     <para>
      This option can only be used when the materialized view is already
      populated.
     </para>
     <para>
      Even with this option only one <literal>REFRESH</literal> at a time may
      run against any one materialized view.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      The name (optionally schema-qualified) of the materialized view to
      refresh.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>WHERE</literal> <replaceable class="parameter">condition</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      A <literal>WHERE</literal> clause specifying a condition that determines
      which rows to refresh. The condition applies to both the existing data
      in the materialized view and the new data generated by the view's defining query.
     </para>

     <warning>
      <para>
       A partial refresh recomputes only the rows that
       <replaceable class="parameter">condition</replaceable> selects.  If the
       correct value of any other row depends on the rows being refreshed, that
       row is left stale, and no error or warning is raised.  The condition must
       therefore cover every row whose <emphasis>output</emphasis> changes, not
       merely the rows whose input changed.
      </para>
      <para>
       Window functions are the usual way to get this wrong.  In a materialized
       view defined with <literal>rank() OVER (PARTITION BY region ORDER BY
       score DESC)</literal>, changing one score changes the rank of every other
       row in that region, so refreshing by the player's own key leaves the rest
       of the region wrong while reporting success.  The condition has to select
       the whole window partition.  Running totals, percentages of a total or of
       a maximum, <literal>ORDER BY</literal> with <literal>LIMIT</literal>, and
       recursive queries all behave the same way; for a recursive query the
       affected set is not derivable from the changed rows at all.  A query
       whose only aggregation is <literal>GROUP BY</literal> is the safe case,
       because a group's value depends on nothing outside that group.
      </para>
      <para>
       Whether the condition can be pushed down to the tables the defining query
       reads is a good proxy for whether it is the right scope, and
       <command>EXPLAIN</command> on the defining query with the condition
       applied will show it.  A condition on a <literal>GROUP BY</literal> key or
       on a <literal>PARTITION BY</literal> key reaches the base tables; one on
       an aggregate or window result, or through <literal>LIMIT</literal>, does
       not.  A condition that does not push down is usually also the wrong
       scope, so the efficient choice and the correct one tend to coincide.
       Note that this is a proxy and not a proof: a condition can push down
       perfectly and still be too narrow, which is what happens when the
       defining query computes a share of a total in a scalar subquery.
      </para>
     </warning>

     <para>
      A row that <replaceable class="parameter">condition</replaceable> selects
      but that the defining query no longer produces is deleted from the
      materialized view.  This is what makes a partial refresh converge with a
      full one, but it can surprise a caller whose condition names a column that
      the row itself can move on: refreshing
      <literal>WHERE status = 'open'</literal> after a row's status has become
      <literal>'closed'</literal> removes that row rather than updating it.
      Name both the old and the new value, or write the condition on the columns
      of the unique index, which identify the row and so cannot move.
     </para>

     <para>
      If the defining query's result is not a function of the base data alone —
      <literal>DISTINCT ON</literal> or <literal>LIMIT</literal> without an
      <literal>ORDER BY</literal> that breaks every tie, for instance — then a
      partial refresh may produce a different, equally valid answer from the one
      a full refresh would produce, because it plans a different query.  Two
      full refreshes may also disagree in that case, but a partial refresh
      reaches it far more often.
     </para>
     <para>
      Two implementations are available, chosen by whether
      <literal>CONCURRENTLY</literal> is specified.  Both allow concurrent
      reads throughout, and both require a usable unique index and an already
      populated materialized view.
     </para>
     <para>
      With <literal>CONCURRENTLY</literal>, rows are modified in place with an
      upsert under a <literal>ROW EXCLUSIVE</literal> lock.  Refreshes whose
      predicates cover disjoint rows run in parallel; those that overlap
      serialize on row locks.  As with ordinary row-level locking, two
      transactions that refresh overlapping sets of rows in different orders can
      deadlock, and are reported as such.  This form cannot apply a change that
      would require deleting one row before inserting another to satisfy a
      second unique index; use the form below for that.
     </para>
     <para>
      Without <literal>CONCURRENTLY</literal>, the rows matching the condition
      are compared against freshly computed data and the differences applied,
      under an <literal>EXCLUSIVE</literal> lock.  Partial refreshes of the same
      materialized view therefore serialize with each other, and cannot
      deadlock.
     </para>
     <para>
      A partial refresh is only proportional to the number of rows it touches if
      the condition can use an index on <emphasis>both</emphasis> the
      materialized view and the tables its defining query reads.  A condition on
      a column with no index on either side forces a scan of the whole
      materialized view or of the base tables, so the refresh costs as much as a
      full one however few rows it matches.  For the same reason a condition on a
      grouping column of an aggregating query can be pushed down to the base
      tables, while one on an aggregate result cannot.  Passing a set of key
      values as an array is likewise much cheaper than referring to another table
      in the condition, which cannot be pushed down.
     </para>
     <para>
      A partial refresh costs several times more per row than a full rebuild
      does, so beyond some fraction of the materialized view rebuilding it
      outright is faster.  The two forms differ here: with
      <literal>CONCURRENTLY</literal> the crossover is around a tenth of the
      materialized view, and without it around a quarter.  Both figures depend
      on the defining query and on how many indexes the materialized view
      carries, so measure rather than assume.
     </para>
     <para>
      The <literal>WHERE</literal> clause cannot contain volatile functions or
      aggregates.  It is evaluated with the privileges of the owner of the
      materialized view, so a caller who is not the owner may only use
      expressions whose functions are all leakproof.  For the same reason it is
      analyzed under the restricted <xref linkend="guc-search-path"/> that
      applies while the command runs, so any object it names must be
      schema-qualified.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   If there is an <literal>ORDER BY</literal> clause in the materialized
   view's defining query, the original contents of the materialized view
   will be ordered that way; but <command>REFRESH MATERIALIZED
   VIEW</command> does not guarantee to preserve that ordering.
  </para>

  <para>
   While <command>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW</command> is running, the <xref
   linkend="guc-search-path"/> is temporarily changed to <literal>pg_catalog,
   pg_temp</literal>.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   This command will replace the contents of the materialized view called
   <literal>order_summary</literal> using the query from the materialized
   view's definition, and leave it in a scannable state:
<programlisting>
REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW order_summary;
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   This command will free storage associated with the materialized view
   <literal>annual_statistics_basis</literal> and leave it in an unscannable
   state:
<programlisting>
REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW annual_statistics_basis WITH NO DATA;
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   This command will update only the rows in the materialized view
   <literal>order_summary</literal> where the <literal>order_date</literal>
   is in the year 2024:
<programlisting>
REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW order_summary WHERE order_date >= '2024-01-01' AND order_date &lt; '2025-01-01';
</programlisting></para>

 </refsect1>

 <refsect1>
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   <command>REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW</command> is a
   <productname>PostgreSQL</productname> extension.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

